From a6fcecb8296a0f34c1c17aa3b59cbb1e4e4ee793 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 23:00:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Verify kind cluster health before reuse, pin node image,
 declare the Codespaces limitation

Running deploy-to-kind.sh for real for the first time (PR #30 shipped the
manifests but declared they had never been executed) surfaced two things.

The environment one: kind cannot bootstrap a control plane inside a GitHub
Codespace. Seven attempts eliminated inotify limits, CPU/memory (2 -> 4 cores,
8 -> 16GB), disk, kind's version, Kubernetes' version (v1.31/v1.34/v1.37) and
finally this repo's own configuration -- a bare cluster created with no config
at all fails identically, which is what exonerates kind-config.yaml and the
manifests. Documented as a limitation in ARCHITECTURE.md section 13, with the
full attempt table in infra/k8s/README.md, plus a status note at the top of
that README so a reviewer is never misled into thinking this ran.

The real bug, unrelated to the environment: the script's idempotency guard
checked only that a cluster with the right NAME was listed, and treated that
as "reuse it". kind aborts before installing its CNI, so a half-created
cluster is listed while having no pod network and no Ready node -- the script
would have built five images and applied every manifest against an unusable
node, failing much later with a far more confusing error. It now verifies a
Ready node, and on a broken cluster refuses to continue and prints the
inspect/recreate commands rather than deleting anything.

Also pins kindest/node:v1.34.0 so a reviewer gets the Kubernetes this was
written against instead of whatever kind's newest release ships.

