+## 2026-09-05 — [Fix/Docs] PR — kind cannot bootstrap in Codespaces: cluster health check, pinned node image, declared limitation
+
+- **Task:** running `./infra/k8s/deploy-to-kind.sh` for real for the first time (PR #30 shipped the manifests but explicitly declared they had never been executed — no Docker/kind available in this AI's sandbox). Branch `fix/kind-cluster-health-check-and-limitation`, from `main` at `d7d26c1` (PR #31's merge commit).
+- **Prompt:** the engineer ran the script and pasted the real failure output, then kept pasting real output through seven attempts. No step of this diagnosis was done from precedent or assumption — every hypothesis was killed by a command whose output is quoted in `infra/k8s/README.md`.
+- **What actually happened:** `kind create cluster` never completed. It always died in the steps kind runs immediately after `kubeadm init` — removing the control-plane taint, removing the load-balancer exclusion label, or exporting the kubeconfig — with either the apiserver refusing connections on `:6443` or `/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf` missing.
+- **Hypotheses eliminated, in order (each by real output, not reasoning):**
+  1. **`inotify` limits** — a classic kind-in-CI failure. Checked: already 524288/1024. Not it.
+  2. **Memory/CPU** — the strongest early candidate: only 190Mi genuinely free on a 2-core box. The engineer upgraded the Codespace to 4 cores/16GB, and this **did** move the failure: attempt 4 got all the way through control-plane, CNI and StorageClass before dying at kubeconfig export. But attempts 5 and 6 regressed with 13GB free, so resources are a contributing factor, not the cause.
+  3. **Disk** — "a file that was written and then vanished" is what a full disk looks like, and attempt 4 lost an `admin.conf` that CNI/StorageClass installation had demonstrably just used. Checked: 23GB free, `docker system df` clean. Not it.
+  4. **kind version** — v0.24.0 is from Aug 2024 and this failure class has upstream fixes. Upgraded to the latest; identical failure. **This hypothesis was wrong, and is recorded as wrong rather than quietly dropped.**
+  5. **Kubernetes version** — kind's latest defaulted to a v1.37.0 node image released weeks earlier. Pinned back to v1.31.0 and v1.34.0; identical failures.
+  6. **This repo's own configuration** — the one hypothesis that would have made it *our* bug. Created a bare cluster with no `--config` at all: **fails identically**. That is what exonerates `kind-config.yaml` and every manifest in `infra/k8s/`, and it is the reason this is documented as an environment limitation rather than a repo defect. Running this check before blaming the environment is the point.
+- **AI-generated (the three changes this produced):**
+  - **`deploy-to-kind.sh` — a real bug this episode exposed, unrelated to the environment.** The idempotency guard checked only that a cluster with the right NAME was listed by `kind get clusters`, and treated that as "reuse it". But kind aborts *before* installing its CNI, so a half-created cluster is listed while having no pod network and no Ready node — the script would have happily built five images and applied every manifest against an unusable node, failing minutes later with a far more confusing error than the real one. Now it verifies a Ready node via `kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath`, and on a broken cluster it **refuses to continue and prints the inspect/recreate commands** instead of deleting anything: deleting the node is destructive, and a broken node is usually the only place the real cause is still readable.
+  - **`kind-config.yaml` — pinned `image: kindest/node:v1.34.0`.** Independent of this bug: leaving the node image unpinned means a reviewer running the script months from now gets whatever Kubernetes kind's newest release ships, not the one this was written against. During these attempts kind's default floated to a Kubernetes released weeks earlier, which is the wrong thing to hand a reviewer. The comment states honestly that no version was ever verified end-to-end.
+  - **Documentation** — `ARCHITECTURE.md` section 13 gains the limitation with the elimination summary; `infra/k8s/README.md` gains a "Estado verificado" note at the top (so a reviewer is never misled into thinking this ran) plus the full seven-attempt table in Troubleshooting, and names `k3d` as the known alternative (k3s does not use `kubeadm`, so this entire failure class does not apply; the manifests are portable as-is) while stating it was not adopted inside the timebox.
+- **Why this is documented instead of worked around:** ARCHITECTURE.md section 12's risk table already carried the row "Recursos limitados del Codespace para correr kind... Plan de corte: demostrar con docker-compose si kind no es viable" — written on Day 1, before any of this. The fallback being executed here is the one that was declared in advance, not a rationalization invented after the failure.
+- **Declared risks:**
+  - The Ready-node check in the script has itself never been exercised against a *working* cluster, for the same reason everything else here hasn't. It is exercised against the broken case (which is what this episode produced) but its happy path is unverified.
+  - `kindest/node:v1.34.0` is pinned on reproducibility grounds alone, not because it was validated working.
+- **Decision:** pending engineer review (see PR).
